1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,464
This is ECE 358, video 15a. The topic is subnet design.

2
00:00:06,914 --> 00:00:26,733
After this video, you can split a block of addresses into subnets of the correct size. You can give the network ID, the prefix and the broadcast address of each subnet. Then we solve question 47, and question 46, which was on the Fall 2024 final exam.

3
00:00:27,183 --> 00:00:39,641
Here is the subnetting example of slide 38. A company gets the IP block 192.168.16.0/24.

4
00:00:40,091 --> 00:00:46,769
The prefix is slash 24. So 32 minus 24, which is 8 bits, are host bits.

5
00:00:47,219 --> 00:01:04,904
8 host bits give 2 to the power 8, which is 256 addresses: from 192.168.16.0 to 192.168.16.255.

6
00:01:05,354 --> 00:01:17,919
The company has four departments, and each department needs its own LAN. LAN A has 120 hosts, LAN B has 50, LAN C has 12, and LAN D has 28.

7
00:01:18,369 --> 00:01:27,436
The task: give the network ID and the prefix of each LAN. As a network engineer, you must use the given addresses efficiently.

8
00:01:27,886 --> 00:01:38,617
Step 1: find the size of each LAN. A LAN needs one address for each host, one address for the network ID, and one for the broadcast address.

9
00:01:39,067 --> 00:01:46,939
LAN A has 120 hosts. It needs 120 plus 2, which is 122 addresses.

10
00:01:47,389 --> 00:02:01,682
A block size must be a power of 2. 64 is less than 122, and 128 is enough. So LAN A gets 128 addresses, which is 2 to the power 7.

11
00:02:02,132 --> 00:02:09,215
So LAN A has 7 host bits. Its prefix is 32 minus 7, which is slash 25.

12
00:02:09,665 --> 00:02:15,190
LAN B has 50 hosts. It needs 50 plus 2, which is 52 addresses.

13
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:24,899
32 is less than 52, and 64 is enough. So LAN B gets 64 addresses, which is 2 to the power 6.

14
00:02:25,349 --> 00:02:32,154
LAN B has 6 host bits. Its prefix is 32 minus 6, which is slash 26.

15
00:02:32,604 --> 00:02:38,065
LAN C has 12 hosts. It needs 12 plus 2, which is 14 addresses.

16
00:02:38,515 --> 00:02:46,707
8 is less than 14, and 16 is enough. So LAN C gets 16 addresses, which is 2 to the power 4.

17
00:02:47,157 --> 00:02:54,005
LAN C has 4 host bits. Its prefix is 32 minus 4, which is slash 28.

18
00:02:54,455 --> 00:03:00,215
LAN D has 28 hosts. It needs 28 plus 2, which is 30 addresses.

19
00:03:00,665 --> 00:03:09,156
16 is less than 30, and 32 is enough. So LAN D gets 32 addresses, which is 2 to the power 5.

20
00:03:09,606 --> 00:03:16,710
LAN D has 5 host bits. Its prefix is 32 minus 5, which is slash 27.

21
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:22,600
The dashed end of each bar shows the addresses that the LAN gets but does not use.

22
00:03:23,050 --> 00:03:32,629
The rule: assign the smallest power of 2 that holds the need. If the block has h host bits, the prefix is 32 minus h.

23
00:03:33,079 --> 00:03:42,721
Step 2: place the blocks in the address range. Only the last byte changes. It goes from 0 to 255, from left to right.

24
00:03:43,171 --> 00:03:58,297
Start with the largest block. LAN A has 128 addresses. It takes the first 128 addresses: from 0 to 0 plus 128 minus 1, which is 127.

25
00:03:58,747 --> 00:04:13,829
The next largest block is LAN B, with 64 addresses. It starts at the next free address, 128. It ends at 128 plus 64 minus 1, which is 191.

26
00:04:14,279 --> 00:04:26,546
Next is LAN D, with 32 addresses. It starts at 192, and it ends at 192 plus 32 minus 1, which is 223.

27
00:04:26,996 --> 00:04:38,708
Then LAN C, with 16 addresses. It starts at 224, and it ends at 224 plus 16 minus 1, which is 239.

28
00:04:39,158 --> 00:04:47,798
The four LANs use 128 plus 64 plus 32 plus 16, which is 240 addresses.

29
00:04:48,248 --> 00:04:59,171
256 minus 240 is 16. So 16 addresses, from 240 to 255, are free for later.

30
00:04:59,621 --> 00:05:09,967
The rule: place the largest block first. Each next block starts at the next free address. The diagram on slide 39 shows the same split.

31
00:05:10,417 --> 00:05:19,335
Why do we place the largest block first? Look at a bad order. LAN C takes the first 16 addresses, 0 to 15.

32
00:05:19,785 --> 00:05:24,606
Then LAN A, with 128 addresses, would start at 16.

33
00:05:25,056 --> 00:05:41,077
Check the address 192.168.16.16/25. With slash 25, the last 7 bits are host bits. In a network ID, all host bits must be 0.

34
00:05:41,527 --> 00:06:00,109
16 in binary is 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 0. The bit with weight 16 is a host bit, and it is 1. So 192.168.16.16 is not a network ID for slash 25.

35
00:06:00,559 --> 00:06:10,649
So a block of 2 to the power h addresses must start at a multiple of 2 to the power h. Then all its h host bits are 0 at the start.

36
00:06:11,099 --> 00:06:20,273
Check our design. LAN A starts at 0, and 0 is 0 times 128. Its 7 host bits are all 0.

37
00:06:20,723 --> 00:06:28,232
LAN B starts at 128, which is 2 times 64. Its 6 host bits are all 0.

38
00:06:28,682 --> 00:06:36,085
LAN D starts at 192, which is 6 times 32. Its 5 host bits are all 0.

39
00:06:36,535 --> 00:06:44,215
LAN C starts at 224, which is 14 times 16. Its 4 host bits are all 0.

40
00:06:44,665 --> 00:06:55,267
When you place the largest block first, the next free address is always a multiple of the next block size. So every block is aligned, and there is no gap.

41
00:06:55,717 --> 00:07:07,899
Step 3: complete the table of slide 39. For each LAN, the network ID is the first address of its block. The broadcast address is the last address of its block.

42
00:07:08,349 --> 00:07:21,383
LAN A starts at 0 and has the prefix slash 25. So its network ID is 192.168.16.0/25.

43
00:07:21,833 --> 00:07:38,793
Its last address is 0 plus 128 minus 1, which is 127. So its broadcast address is 192.168.16.127. The slide gives this row.

44
00:07:39,243 --> 00:07:51,446
LAN B starts at 128. Its network ID is 192.168.16.128/26.

45
00:07:51,896 --> 00:08:06,957
Its last address is 128 plus 64 minus 1, which is 191. Its broadcast address is 192.168.16.191.

46
00:08:07,407 --> 00:08:19,695
LAN C starts at 224. Its network ID is 192.168.16.224/28.

47
00:08:20,145 --> 00:08:33,585
224 plus 16 minus 1 is 239. Its broadcast address is 192.168.16.239.

48
00:08:34,035 --> 00:08:46,217
LAN D starts at 192. Its network ID is 192.168.16.192/27.

49
00:08:46,667 --> 00:09:00,384
192 plus 32 minus 1 is 223. Its broadcast address is 192.168.16.223.

50
00:09:00,834 --> 00:09:11,415
Check the design in binary. Only the last byte changes. LAN A is slash 25, so 1 bit of the last byte is in the prefix. That bit is 0.

51
00:09:11,865 --> 00:09:19,140
The 7 host bits go from all 0 to all 1. That is .0 to .127.

52
00:09:19,590 --> 00:09:25,499
LAN B is slash 26. Its 2 prefix bits in the last byte are 1 0.

53
00:09:25,949 --> 00:09:34,845
Its 6 host bits go from all 0 to all 1. So LAN B covers .128 to .191.

54
00:09:35,295 --> 00:09:40,394
LAN D is slash 27. Its 3 prefix bits are 1 1 0.

55
00:09:40,844 --> 00:09:46,135
So LAN D covers .192 to .223.

56
00:09:46,585 --> 00:09:51,769
LAN C is slash 28. Its 4 prefix bits are 1 1 1 0.

57
00:09:52,219 --> 00:09:57,445
So LAN C covers .224 to .239.

58
00:09:57,895 --> 00:10:03,975
The free block is also a slash 28 block. Its prefix bits are 1 1 1 1.

59
00:10:04,425 --> 00:10:09,631
So the free block covers .240 to .255.

60
00:10:10,081 --> 00:10:18,507
No prefix is the start of another prefix. So the blocks do not overlap, and each address belongs to exactly one LAN.

61
00:10:18,957 --> 00:10:35,917
Question 47 is the same company, with a fifth department. LAN E has 10 hosts. For each subnet, find the block size, the network ID, the prefix length, the broadcast address, and one host IP address. Pause the video and try it.

62
00:10:36,367 --> 00:10:50,106
LANs A to D do not change. Use the same blocks as before: A at .0, B at .128, D at .192, and C at .224.

63
00:10:50,556 --> 00:10:55,697
LAN E has 10 hosts. It needs 10 plus 2, which is 12 addresses.

64
00:10:56,147 --> 00:11:05,534
8 is less than 12, and 16 is enough. So LAN E gets 16 addresses, which is 2 to the power 4. That is 4 host bits.

65
00:11:05,984 --> 00:11:18,635
The free block from .240 to .255 has 256 minus 240, which is 16 addresses. So LAN E fits there exactly.

66
00:11:19,085 --> 00:11:37,175
The table marks the network ID, the prefix and the broadcast of E with x, so the question does not ask for them. They would be 192.168.16.240/28 and .255.

67
00:11:37,625 --> 00:11:55,716
For one host IP address, take any address between the network ID and the broadcast address. Here, take the network ID plus 1: .1, .129, .225, .193 and .241.

68
00:11:56,166 --> 00:12:04,102
240 plus 16 is 256. So now all 256 addresses are used.

69
00:12:04,552 --> 00:12:20,765
Question 46 was on the Fall 2024 final exam. A router connects three subnets. All their interfaces must use the prefix 223.1.17.0/24.

70
00:12:21,215 --> 00:12:28,917
Subnet 1 needs at least 60 interfaces, subnet 2 at least 90, and subnet 3 at least 12.

71
00:12:29,367 --> 00:12:34,508
A future subnet 4 must get a maximal part of the remaining addresses.

72
00:12:34,958 --> 00:12:57,358
The table gives one IP address in each subnet: 223.1.17.140, 223.1.17.75, 223.1.17.197, and 223.1.17.230.

73
00:12:57,808 --> 00:13:03,696
Find the four network addresses, their network IDs and their broadcast addresses.

74
00:13:04,146 --> 00:13:12,167
Then find the largest number of IP addresses that a future subnet 5 can have. Pause the video and try it.

75
00:13:12,617 --> 00:13:24,521
Step 1: the block sizes. Subnet 1 needs at least 60 interfaces, plus the network ID, plus the broadcast address. That is 60 plus 2, which is 62.

76
00:13:24,971 --> 00:13:34,486
32 is less than 62, and 64 is enough. So subnet 1 gets 64 addresses, which is 2 to the power 6.

77
00:13:34,936 --> 00:13:40,419
6 host bits give a prefix of 32 minus 6, which is slash 26.

78
00:13:40,869 --> 00:13:44,517
Subnet 2 needs 90 plus 2, which is 92.

79
00:13:44,967 --> 00:13:55,911
64 is less than 92, and 128 is enough. So subnet 2 gets 128 addresses, which is 2 to the power 7.

80
00:13:56,361 --> 00:14:01,907
7 host bits give a prefix of 32 minus 7, which is slash 25.

81
00:14:02,357 --> 00:14:05,771
Subnet 3 needs 12 plus 2, which is 14.

82
00:14:06,221 --> 00:14:14,605
8 is less than 14, and 16 is enough. So subnet 3 gets 16 addresses, which is 2 to the power 4.

83
00:14:15,055 --> 00:14:20,495
4 host bits give a prefix of 32 minus 4, which is slash 28.

84
00:14:20,945 --> 00:14:30,267
The router interface is already one of the interfaces that the question counts. So we add only the network ID and the broadcast address.

85
00:14:30,717 --> 00:14:51,667
Step 2: the given address fixes where each block is. Subnet 1 is slash 26 and contains 223.1.17.140. In binary, 140 is 128 plus 8 plus 4: 1 0 0 0 1 1 0 0.

86
00:14:52,117 --> 00:15:07,349
Slash 26 has 26 prefix bits. The first 24 are 223.1.17. So 26 minus 24, which is 2 bits of the last byte, are prefix bits: 1 0.

87
00:15:07,799 --> 00:15:25,036
The network ID has all host bits 0. Keep 1 0, and clear the other 6 bits. The result is 128. So subnet 1 is 223.1.17.128/26.

88
00:15:25,486 --> 00:15:41,635
For the broadcast address, set the 6 host bits to 1. That is 128 plus 63, which is 191. So the broadcast address is 223.1.17.191.

89
00:15:42,085 --> 00:15:55,824
Subnet 2 is slash 25 and contains .75. In binary, 75 is 64 plus 8 plus 2 plus 1: 0 1 0 0 1 0 1 1.

90
00:15:56,274 --> 00:16:02,759
25 minus 24 is 1. So 1 bit of the last byte is a prefix bit: 0.

91
00:16:03,209 --> 00:16:15,177
Keep the 0, and clear the other 7 bits. The result is 0. So subnet 2 is 223.1.17.0/25.

92
00:16:15,627 --> 00:16:29,793
Set the 7 host bits to 1: 0 plus 127 is 127. The broadcast address is 223.1.17.127.

93
00:16:30,243 --> 00:16:46,307
Subnet 3 is slash 28 and contains .197. In binary, 197 is 128 plus 64 plus 4 plus 1: 1 1 0 0 0 1 0 1.

94
00:16:46,757 --> 00:16:54,671
28 minus 24 is 4. So 4 bits of the last byte are prefix bits: 1 1 0 0.

95
00:16:55,121 --> 00:17:09,799
Keep 1 1 0 0, and clear the other 4 bits. The result is 192. So subnet 3 is 223.1.17.192/28.

96
00:17:10,249 --> 00:17:23,326
Set the 4 host bits to 1: 192 plus 15 is 207. The broadcast address is 223.1.17.207.

97
00:17:23,776 --> 00:17:39,584
Now draw the range of the last byte. Subnet 2 uses 0 to 127. Subnet 1 uses 128 to 191. Subnet 3 uses 192 to 207.

98
00:17:40,034 --> 00:17:49,741
The free addresses are 208 to 255. That is 256 minus 208, which is 48 addresses.

99
00:17:50,191 --> 00:18:04,975
Step 3: subnet 4 must keep a maximal part of the free addresses. They go from 208 to 255. That is 256 minus 208, which is 48 addresses.

100
00:18:05,425 --> 00:18:14,662
48 is not a power of 2. It is between 32 and 64. So one subnet cannot take all 48 addresses.

101
00:18:15,112 --> 00:18:30,557
A block of 32 must start at a multiple of 32. 224 is 7 times 32, but 208 is 6.5 times 32. So a block of 32 cannot start at 208.

102
00:18:31,007 --> 00:18:39,690
So the largest aligned block inside this range has 32 addresses: 224 to 255.

103
00:18:40,140 --> 00:18:54,391
The given address of subnet 4 is .230. It is inside 224 to 255. So subnet 4 is this block of 32 addresses, with 5 host bits.

104
00:18:54,841 --> 00:19:13,187
In binary, 230 is 128 plus 64 plus 32 plus 4 plus 2: 1 1 1 0 0 1 1 0. Slash 27 has 27 minus 24, which is 3 prefix bits in the last byte: 1 1 1.

105
00:19:13,637 --> 00:19:38,811
Clear the host bits: 1 1 1 0 0 0 0 0 is 224. So the network ID is 223.1.17.224/27. Set the host bits to 1: 255. So the broadcast address is 223.1.17.255.

106
00:19:39,261 --> 00:19:54,066
The addresses 208 to 223 are left. That is 224 minus 208, which is 16 addresses. And 208 is 13 times 16, so the block is aligned.

107
00:19:54,516 --> 00:20:09,599
So a future subnet 5 can have at most 16 IP addresses, counting its network ID and broadcast address. It is 223.1.17.208/28.

108
00:20:10,049 --> 00:20:43,578
Here is the full answer. The four network addresses are 223.1.17.128/26 for subnet 1, 223.1.17.0/25 for subnet 2, 223.1.17.192/28 for subnet 3, and 223.1.17.224/27 for subnet 4.

109
00:20:44,028 --> 00:20:53,820
Their broadcast addresses are .191, .127, .207 and .255.

110
00:20:54,270 --> 00:21:05,235
A future subnet 5 can have at most 16 IP addresses: 223.1.17.208/28.

111
00:21:05,685 --> 00:21:09,461
In the exam, show the binary work for each given address.

112
00:21:09,911 --> 00:21:24,525
Here is the recap. To size a subnet, add 2 to the number of hosts, for the network ID and the broadcast address. Take the smallest power of 2 that holds it. The prefix is 32 minus the host bits.

113
00:21:24,975 --> 00:21:33,209
Place the largest block first. A block of 2 to the power h addresses starts at a multiple of 2 to the power h.

114
00:21:33,659 --> 00:21:52,006
If an address in the subnet is given, set the host bits to 0 for the network ID, and to 1 for the broadcast address. For leftover space, take the largest aligned block. In video 15b, we see how a host gets its address with DHCP.
